Subject: [PATCH 8/8] terminal: Hide a host program's own input chrome behind
 the composer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When the input composer is open, a full-screen TUI keeps drawing its own
input box and status line pinned to the bottom of the grid, duplicating the
composer. Grow the PTY by a fixed strip of rows beyond the visible area and
let the terminal's `overflow_hidden` clip them: the program keeps drawing
that chrome off-screen behind the composer while output stays at the top,
untouched. The strip is positional, so it holds while scrolling.

Submitting also folds a trailing carriage return into the same write as the
text, which such a program treats as a newline in its own box rather than a
submit — forcing a second manual Return. Follow the text with a lone
carriage return on the next tick so it lands as a discrete Enter keystroke
and the command runs from a single Return in the composer.

Release Notes:

- Improved the terminal input editor: a host TUI's redundant input box is
  hidden behind the composer, and a single Return submits the command.
